<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Venus de Milo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 150–125 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Aphrodite of Milos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Venus de Milo, or Aphrodite of Milos, is an ancient Greek marble statue of a goddess, usually identified as Aphrodite, found on the island of Melos in 1820. Made in the late second century BCE and probably the work of Alexandros of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, it is about two metres tall and has lost both arms. It has been in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> since 1821.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/venus-de-milo.jpg" alt="Venus de Milo" width="320" height="602" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Livioandronico2013 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Front_views_of_the_Venus_de_Milo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
